 "title": "How search finds one word: inverted index",
 "sources": [
  "Wikipedia, 'Inverted index' (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inverted_index, fetched 2026-11-24, secondary source): 'a database index storing a mapping from content, such as words or numbers, to its locations in a table, or in a document or a set of documents'; 'the most popular data structure used in document retrieval systems'; fast full-text search 'at a cost of increased processing when a document is added to the database'.",
  "Illustration only: 'redis' -> documents 3, 7, 9 and the two-word intersection are an example written for this video."
 ],
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "A billion documents, and search finds the word you typed almost instantly. Does it read every document? Guess.",
   "caps": "A *BILLION DOCS | ALMOST *INSTANTLY | READ *EVERY ONE?"
  },
  {
   "text": "No. It reads them once, ahead of time, and builds an inverted index.",
   "caps": "*NO | READ ONCE, *AHEAD | *INVERTED INDEX"
  },
  {
   "text": "Like the index at the back of a book, it maps each word to the documents that contain it.",
   "caps": "EACH *WORD | TO ITS *DOCUMENTS"
  },
  {
   "text": "So redis points to documents three, seven and nine. Search just looks up the word.",
   "caps": "*REDIS: 3, 7, 9 | *LOOK IT UP"
  },
  {
   "text": "Two words? Take both lists, and keep the documents they share.",
   "caps": "*TWO WORDS | *SHARED DOCS"
  },
  {
   "text": "But here's the catch: every new document has to be added to the index first.",
   "caps": "THE *CATCH | NEW DOCS *INDEXED FIRST"
  },
  {
   "text": "So writes cost extra work, and that's the price of fast searches.",
   "caps": "WRITES COST *MORE | FOR *FAST SEARCH"
  },
  {
   "text": "That's how search engines do it. Did you guess it reads everything?",
   "caps": "THAT'S *SEARCH | DID YOU GUESS *RIGHT?"
  }
 ],
